+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static homepage with a browser-only encrypted delivery note preview.
+
+Run `pnpm install`, `pnpm build`, and `pnpm test`. Deploy the `dist/` directory over HTTPS. The `start` script is configured for Vite so browser dependencies are resolved; bundled production assets are in `dist`.
+
+## Encryption and integration boundary
+
+The default suite uses Noble's X-Wing hybrid KEM (ML-KEM-768 + X25519). Standalone ML-KEM-768 and classical ephemeral-static X25519 are also selectable; classical mode is explicitly not post-quantum protected. Bundled Noble implementations provide compatibility without relying on emerging native browser KEM APIs. AES-256-GCM and HKDF-SHA-256 use native Web Crypto; encryption is disabled when unavailable.
+
+Each encryption uses a fresh encapsulation or ephemeral key, HKDF salt, and 96-bit GCM IV. A versioned, fixed-order header (including recipient fingerprint, suite, record ID, capsule, salt, and IV) is authenticated as additional data. All note content and delivery preferences are encrypted. There is no network submission or browser storage. The generated local recipient private key stays in memory for preview decryption and is discarded on delete, replacement, or navigation. Byte arrays are zeroed where practical; JavaScript cannot guarantee memory erasure.
+
+This is a local working preview, not a connected delivery system. To integrate a real service, provision the recipient keys outside the customer browser, authenticate and pin/version the recipient public key delivered over HTTPS, call `encryptNote` with that public key, and send only the resulting envelope to an authorized backend. Only the authorized delivery recipient should hold the private key and perform decapsulation/decryption. Define order authorization, key rotation/revocation, retention, and deletion before accepting real notes. A KEM alone does not authenticate the identity of a recipient. Do not use locally generated preview recipient keys for real deliveries.
+
+Copied envelopes cannot be decrypted after the preview tab's recipient key is discarded. The UI explains this session-only lifetime. Notes are rendered using `textContent`; no plaintext is logged, persisted, or placed in a URL. Client encryption does not protect against compromised page scripts or a compromised recipient device. External Google Fonts and Unsplash images are visual assets only and receive no note content; the page sets a no-referrer policy.
